<description>
SQLite statements are C objects which can be long lived, so having them be initialized in a constructor, and cleanup everything in a destructor, makes the sqlite code cleaner and easier to read. This also lets us have the various functions needed to interact with sqlite statements be member functions.

This will be particularly useful in future work which makes use of more complicated SQL statements.
</description>

<commits>
3a6a36f9c3 sqlite: Add SQLiteStatement RAII class

This class will be used to encapsulate a sqlite3_stmt

a483a874a9 sqlite: Make Column template function

528aade871 sqlite: Refactor ReadPragmaInteger to use SQLiteStatement

c2817f0f9a sqlite: Use SQLiteStatement in PRAGMA integrity_check

eda29df20e sqlite: Use SQLiteStatement in check_main_stmt

98fc446765 sqlite: Have SQLiteCursor store SQLiteStatement

ed5f071513 sqlite: Replace remaining sqlite3_stmt usage with SQLiteStatement

4927b6f769 sqlite: Refactor common writing code from WriteKey and ExecStatement

WriteKey and ExecStatement use the same code for the actual execution of
the statement.

eb4cbc10e5 sqlite: Inline BindBlobToStatement and SpanFromBlob

774fbc89c9 sqlite: Construct SQLiteStatements when needed

Instead of constructing all SQLiteStatements when SQLiteBatch is
constructed, construct them once when they are needed before each read,
write, or erase operation. Once constructed, the statement will persist
for the lifetime of the SQLiteBatch to be reused across multiple
statements.
</commits>

